EXT. LAKESIDE DOCK - LATE SUMMER DUSK

A weathered dock stretches into a still lake. Mosquitoes hang in gold light. At the shore end, a bonfire has burned down to red embers, and six teenagers sit in a ragged arc around it, the last evening of the last summer before everything changes.

JUNIPER "JUNE" BAPTISTE, 17, sits at the dock's far end with her feet in the water, a paperback face-down beside her. She watches the horizon as though waiting for it to say something.

At the fire, MALCOLM OKONKWO-REYES, 18, strums a guitar missing a string. NADIA FARHAT, 17, sprawls on a cooler, eating marshmallows straight from the bag. BEN "SPUD" KOWALSKI, 16, builds a precarious tower of empty cans. The twins, ROSALIND and ROMILLY PETERS, 16, share a blanket, whispering, identical down to the chipped nail polish.

No one has said what they are all thinking. Every few minutes, someone almost does, and the moment passes.

                    NADIA
(through marshmallow)
We should do something.

                    MALCOLM
We are doing something.

                    NADIA
Sitting is not doing.

                    SPUD
I'm doing architecture.

                    ROSALIND
It's a can tower.

                    SPUD
It's a monument.

                    ROMILLY
To what?

                    SPUD
To us.

Quiet. The guitar strums a flat chord. Somebody laughs, a short and exhausted laugh, and then the embers pop.

June stands. She pads along the dock toward them, wet footprints darkening the boards. She stops at the edge of the firelight.

                    JUNE
I leave Thursday.

Nobody speaks. The tower of cans collapses with a clatter. Spud doesn't flinch.

                    MALCOLM
Thursday?

                    JUNE
My mom moved it up.

                    NADIA
You said Saturday.

                    JUNE
I know what I said.

She sits on the cooler's edge. Takes a marshmallow from Nadia's bag, rolls it in her fingers, doesn't eat it.

                    ROMILLY
Where is it again?

                    JUNE
Portland.

                    ROSALIND
That's not so far.

                    JUNE
It's three states.

                    ROSALIND
That's not so far.

Malcolm sets the guitar aside. He looks at the ground. His hands are trembling a little, and he buries them between his knees.

                    MALCOLM
We could drive up. Visit.

                    JUNE
You don't have a car.

                    MALCOLM
Spud has a car.

                    SPUD
I have a bicycle.

                    NADIA
You have a lawn mower you ride.

                    SPUD
It's a very good lawn mower.

They laugh. It dies quickly. June squeezes the marshmallow into a pale pellet and lets it fall.

                    JUNE
I'm not good at this.

                    ROMILLY
Nobody is.

                    JUNE
I thought I'd have something to say.

                    NADIA
(gently)
You don't have to.

June nods. She wipes her face with the heel of her hand. Rosalind and Romilly lift the blanket and open it. June crawls in between them and they close it again, a single bundle of knees and tangled hair.

Malcolm picks up the guitar. Finds the one chord he knows. Plays it, over and over, not well.

The fire sinks lower. Across the lake, the last light bleeds out of the sky and a single loon calls once. Nobody answers it.
